                                            "summary":  "The piece profiles Ron Baron\u0027s 40-year track record of holding exceptional businesses for the long term, generating over $60 billion in returns. His funds\u0027 outperformance is largely attributed to early, concentrated positions in Tesla (bought at $14/share in 2014) and an ongoing $25 billion stake in SpaceX. Baron\u0027s method centers on backing visionary founders and holding through volatilityÃ¢â‚¬â€a discipline most institutional investors cannot or will not maintainÃ¢â‚¬â€and he now believes SpaceX could become the world\u0027s most valuable company.",
                                            "take":  "Baron\u0027s story is the clearest modern proof that extreme long-termism paired with founder conviction can generate returns that no diversification strategy can replicate. But the survivorship bias here is real: this narrative works when you happen to pick Tesla and SpaceX. The actionable lesson is less about copying his portfolio and more about understanding his processÃ¢â‚¬â€specifically, his willingness to hold through drawdowns that force most managers to sell. The harder question for any investor is whether they can genuinely underwrite that kind of conviction before the outcome is known, or whether they would have sold Tesla in 2019 like almost everyone else did.",
                                            "isNew":  true
