                                                              "summary":  "Damodaran revisits the long-running debate over how interest rates affect equity valuations, noting that despite Treasury yields reaching 20-year highs in 2026, U.S. stock markets have remained resilient. He demonstrates that the rate-equity relationship is more nuanced than simple inverse correlation, because corporate cash flows themselves respond to macroeconomic conditions that drive rates. Robust earnings growth across the S\u0026P 500 has offset the mechanical discount-rate headwind. The piece argues investors must look at rate changes in the context of why rates are moving, not just the direction of movement.",
                                                              "take":  "This is a timely corrective to the simplistic \u0027rates up = stocks down\u0027 narrative that dominated 2022-2023. Damodaran\u0027s point that earnings quality and cash flow resilience matter more than the rate level itself is well-supported by 2026 market data. For investors, the implication is clear: stop waiting for rate cuts as a permission slip to buy equities. If earnings hold, elevated rates are a headwind, not a death sentence. The bigger risk is a growth slowdown that hits both earnings and sentiment simultaneously ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â that scenario, not high rates alone, is what to watch.",
