                                                              "imageUrl":  "https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgh5fXUk3rKQgi_I28N1acK-5yXvwUPErlVboSe6pyCHZL8x0-r5qMMHlHdVhSxhBxFeuLGARgSFuwvCTDO_AdIgPsSjnpLeGDHHWmsXuT9hy7GID8Rkfe5QL_54HQTfOG1JUheL-vNqI2rSUlHl79wOqBiuwCGhvwRaQkpP2pQrPQ_PantALROtViRqHw/s320/Khoslatweet.jpg",
                                                              "summary":  "Damodaran challenges venture capital\u0027s default assumption that startups must prioritize scaling revenue growth over profitability. He argues that VC incentive structures and the influx of public capital into private markets have pushed founders toward unsustainable growth-at-all-costs strategies. Many of the resulting companies carry impressive valuations but fragile underlying economics. The post advocates for context-dependent business strategies that match the company\u0027s actual competitive position rather than following a universal scaling mandate.",
                                                              "take":  "This is one of the more important structural critiques of the VC model in recent years, and it lands with extra weight as the AI investment boom creates a new generation of well-funded but profitability-deferred companies. The argument that \u0027not all businesses are meant to scale\u0027 directly challenges the prevailing AI infrastructure narrative. Investors evaluating AI-adjacent growth stocks should heed this: elevated revenue multiples are only defensible if there is a credible path to margin expansion. Companies burning cash to chase scale in commoditizing AI layers face serious valuation risk when the music slows.",
