                                                              "title":  "We ain\u0027t seen nothing yet.",
                                                              "source":  "Downtown Josh Brown",
                                                              "author":  "Josh Brown",
                                                              "url":  "https://www.downtownjoshbrown.com/p/we-ain-t-seen-nothing-yet",
                                                              "date":  "2026-08-02",
                                                              "imageUrl":  "https://media.beehiiv.com/cdn-cgi/image/fit=scale-down,quality=80,format=auto,onerror=redirect/uploads/asset/file/edc330cd-79d0-4826-a3a5-bf12f41ad6a3/alex_kantrowitz.png?t=1785677394",
                                                              "summary":  "Josh Brown interviews Big Technology journalist Alex Kantrowitz, who argues that current AI compute infrastructure is severely undersized relative to future demand. While Wall Street frets about data center capex overbuilding, industry insiders believe available compute will be woefully insufficient once billions of AI agents, robots, and autonomous systems reach deployment scale. Brown sides with the insiders, contending that markets are mispricing AI infrastructure as a near-term bubble rather than a multi-decade buildout. The piece positions today\u0027s hyperscaler capex as the foundation of a compute supercycle.",
                                                              "take":  "This is a high-conviction bull case for AI infrastructure that deserves serious engagement even if you are skeptical. The key question is timing: if agent-scale AI demand is 5-10 years out, current capex levels may genuinely be overextended relative to near-term monetization. Brown\u0027s framing sidesteps that timing risk. Still, the structural argument is sound ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â the history of transformative technology (railroads, the internet) shows infrastructure is almost always underbuilt in retrospect. The actionable read: maintain exposure to hyperscalers and power infrastructure rather than trying to time a capex peak.",
                                                              "isNew":  true
