                                                              "title":  "Santoli: The youthful phase of AI is over. What it means for investors",
                                                              "source":  "Mike Santoli (CNBC)",
                                                              "author":  "Michael Santoli",
                                                              "url":  "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/09/15/santoli-the-youthful-phase-of-ai-is-over-what-it-means-for-investors.html",
                                                              "date":  "2026-09-15",
                                                              "imageUrl":  null,
                                                              "summary":  "Santoli argues that the early AI era ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â when the technology\u0027s promise was unlimited, winners were obvious, and optimism was reflexively rewarded ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â has ended. The narrative has shifted toward risk: AI is increasingly unpopular with the broader public, revenue projections beyond a few quarters are suspect, and the easy-win trades have closed. He acknowledges that AI profits will still materialize over time but frames the current moment as a transition to a harder, more selective phase of the investment cycle.",
                                                              "take":  "This is the most important investor framing piece of the recent AI cycle. Santoli is right that the \u0027rising tide lifts all boats\u0027 phase is over ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â from here, returns in AI investing will require actual discrimination between companies that can extract durable profit from AI and those riding a theme. The public backlash dimension he raises is underappreciated: regulatory and social pressure could compress monetization timelines for consumer-facing AI applications. For investors, this argues for rotating from broad AI-theme ETFs into selective positions in companies with demonstrated AI revenue ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â not just AI capex. The era of buying the concept is over; now you have to buy the earnings.",
